\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
PADSI
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Container like file and network resources segmentation for the Linux desktop
\end_layout

\begin_layout Right Address
For version 0.9.29
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset toc
LatexCommand tableofcontents

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Context
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
PC endpoints are a major compromise vector as they are usually quite poorly secured and have a network access to most of the resources available internally.
 This situation leads to daily catastrophic enterprises impacts as threat actors easily build upon the compromise of a single PC endpoint.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The PADSI project aims to drastically reduce the risks posed by PC endpoints by isolating network and files accesses within the boundaries of business use cases.
 PADSI also aims at reducing as much as possible the number of elements in the infrastructure in order to limit the attack surface,
 and focuses on simplicity and observability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Use case examples include 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

office work
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (using e-mail,
 working on documents,
 presentations,
 and using business specific applications,
 ...) and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Internet surfing
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (information research,
 public information consultation,
 ...).
 Specifically:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

office work
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 use case,
 the user needs an access to enterprise resources (being on premise or located in external cloud environments) and associated information stored locally on files,
 but has no need for accesses to unrelated services exposed on the Internet;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Internet surfing
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 use case,
 the use needs to access publicly available resources over the Internet,
 but not access to work related resources,
 and neither to work related files.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This very simple isolation concept strongly mitigates the following most prevalent attack vectors:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
credentials phishing,
 where the user typically receives an email,
 clicks a link and is redirected to an attacker controlled server where he is asked to enter his credentials:
 because this attacker controlled server is not in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

office work
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 use case,
 the associated web page cannot be displayed and the user can't provide his credentials.
 A side benefit is the user can easily identify he was victim of a phishing attempt.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
ransomware:
 where a threat actor has compromised the system and is able to execute an encryptor.
 If the compromised program is while in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

office work
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 use case,
 the threat actor has no way of communicating with the compromised PC endpoint and thus can't exfiltrate information nor define encryption keys to encrypt that information
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The threat actor can of course still work 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

blindly
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and destroy information,
 but this is then not a ransomware but a wiper and is another type of threat.
\end_layout

\end_inset

;
 as a side benefit,
 the administrator can easily identify from the logs that the system has been compromised.
 If however,
 the compromised program is while in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Internet surfing
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 use case,
 the threat actor can deploy his ransomware,
 but it can only exfiltrate and encrypt non work related files,
 which makes the attack useless for him and harmless for the user.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
PADSI thus proposes fine grained isolation (implemented as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

containers like
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 zones) to define as many use cases as necessary,
 each use case isolation is defined a 
\series bold
zone
\series default
.
 It is particularly aimed as system administrators where the impacts of a PC endpoint compromise are the highest.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Also,
 because the PADSI system aims at covering many use cases,
 system administrators can provide virtual machines to be used by some users in some the context of some use cases.
 These virtual machines have at least the same restrictions in terms of network and files access as is defined for the use case and can have even more restrictions if deemed necessary.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 as a side benefit,
 because the access to non professional services can be strictly restricted,
 PADSI can act a a very simple yet effective data loss prevention system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Features & requirements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
PADSI's features are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
isolation of user's work (within 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

zones
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) based on filesystem and network filtering;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
support for multiple users logged in at the same time;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
network filtering defined as rules (allow or deny) based on either IP addresses or domains which can be further specialized with transport protocols (TCP or UDP) and port numbers;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
sharing of directories among zones to allow inter-zone files transfer (in a read-write or read-only fashion);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
usage of virtual machines (Windows and Linux) for specific jobs;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
polished UX to help the user understand his environment;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
easy installation,
 configuration and administration,
 and easy integration in existing infrastructures;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As for the requirements,
 they are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a recent Linux OS in which modern kernel namespaces isolation mechanisms are available;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a CPU with virtualization extensions to use virtual machines;
 enough RAM also needs to be anticipated in that case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Missing features
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Some missing features which will eventually be implemented include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
seamless integration of VM's applications windows in the desktop to avoid having to use a VM's own desktop;
\end_layout

