\begin_layout Subsection
High level concepts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before reading the rest of the document or using PADSI,
 the following concepts must be understood.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Zone
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "concepts:zone"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

zone
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is a logical description of a set of file and network resources in which a user's programs are executed in order to performs some work around a use case.
 For example,
 a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

work zone
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 might contain all the work related documents (office documents,
 images,
 credentials,
 passwords safes and the like) and provides access to network file shares,
 e-mail,
 and other business related Web services.
 This zone would be different than the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Internet zone
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 where the user performs public Web searches,
 reads blogs or access social media and would only contain the user's bookmarks and a few downloaded files for local consumption.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From a technological point of view,
 PADSI makes a heavy use of applications network and files isolation to create small isolated 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

container like
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 areas in which programs achieving the same goal are.
 Examples of such isolated areas are typically the user's programs in a zone,
 a virtual machine and its associated visualization program,
 or the network infrastructure used by the programs in a zone.
 PADSI uses the generic term 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
bubble
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to define such areas (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "nsbubble"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 for details).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Also,
 even though the user sees each zone as a unique entity,
 the actual implementation will often lead to several 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bubbles
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 being started to create that illusion:
 some 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bubbles
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 will provide network services,
 some will run virtual machines,
 one or more will run the actual user's programs,
 while some may run Flatpak applications.
 A typical zone will run between 2 and 4 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bubbles
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Bubbles
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 are implemented via the Linux kernel's namespaces.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Execution of programs outside of a zone
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "Execution in the init ns"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Except from a UX point of view,
 allowing the user to execute programs outside of any zone is not a security risk.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The use can easily execute programs outside of any zone either by deliberate configuration (e.g.
 allow the Gnome Terminal to be executed in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init zone
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 like the calculator would be),
 by user configuration (map a keyboard shortcut to execute that same program),
 or directly via the Gnome Shell via the Alt+F2 keyboard shortcut.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Network resource
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A network resource is fundamentally a set of IP addresses or domain names and optionally network protocols and port numbers which can be allowed or denied.
 For example,
 one could allow to SSH into a specific server using its IP address with a rule 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

allow TCP port 22 to 10.1.2.3
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 or deny access to the Youtube video platform with a rule like 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

deny youtube.com.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Virtual machine
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Some of the tools users need may not be available directly on the system because maybe they are not packaged,
 maybe the administrator does not want the software to be installed on the system,
 maybe the software is obsolete,
 maybe it requires Windows,
 or maybe the tool requires administrative privileges.
 For all these reasons (and probably many others),
 PADSI lets administrators create virtual machines (
\series bold
VM
\series default
) and users 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

run
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 them within zones,
 thus with the same (or even greater) isolation from the system as any other program running in the same zone.
 Because of that isolation,
 system administrators can decide (based on risks assessments) to give end user complete control (i.e.
 administrative privileges) over the OS running in the VM.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Virtual machines can run any operating system supported by the 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "QEMU"
target "https://www.qemu.org/"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 emulator,
 but proper files and desktop integration is performed only for Windows and most Linux distributions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 contrary to use usage of traditional virtual machines,
 PADSI is based on the assumption that virtual machines are used as temporary tools instantiated on demand and destroyed immediately after (of course an user can keep using a virtual machine as long as the an administrator will allow for).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Programs running in virtual machines can be given access to the files and directories present in the zone in which they are executed,
 so the user can use the same resources when executing programs in the virtual machine or in the associated zone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this context,
 virtual machines are throwable objects,
 and from a security standpoint,
 this ensures that any compromised virtual machine does not offer a foothold in the infrastructure to the attacker.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Virtual machines's associated concepts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
VM version
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "VM-version"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As stated above,
 virtual machines in PADSI are meant to be short lived (depending of course on the user's use cases),
 which is why PADSI distinguishes a VM's definition as presented section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "VM-conf"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 from all the different versions in terms of actual VM disk image files which can exist at any given time.
 Specifically,
 a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
VM version
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is a set of the the following files
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that these files are treated by PADSI as a whole and evolve simultaneously when VMs are used.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\bar under
sharing the same VM definition
\bar default
 in term of configuration:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a VM disk image file (with the 
\family typewriter
.img
\family default
 extension);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a VM's UEFI's NVRAM 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "OVMF"
target "https://github.com/tianocore/tianocore.github.io/wiki/OVMF"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 file (with the 
\family typewriter
.vars
\family default
 extension);
 and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a VM's information file (with the 
\family typewriter
.infos
\family default
 extension).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus when a VM is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
running
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 PADSI actually uses the files associated to a specific VM version.
 This is done is a way such that the disk space used by the VM versions is kept to a minimum and that users and administrators can do their jobs at the same time (thus without requiring any sort of coordination).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Per user customization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to limit the administrators' work to the minimum (installing a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

VM master
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) while at the same time having per user VM,
 before a user can actually run a VM,
 PADSI imposes a 
\series bold
user customization
\series default
 phase of the VM,
 which,
 at least:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
creates the user in the VM (with the same name and possibly UID/GID as in the host);
 and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
sets up auto login for that user.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The administrator can specialize further the per user VM to install some specific software,
 to activate licenses,
 or for any other need.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
VM's nickname
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As the end user can start several VMs simultaneously,
 he can give each instance a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
nickname
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 which can be anything as long as it starts with a letter and only contains letters,
 numbers and the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

-
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

_
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 characters.
 If no nickname is provided,
 then a randomly generated one will be assigned.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A VM's nickname is also used to keep track of VMs between successive runs in case the user needs to stop a VM and restart it at a later time,
 and a VM can be joined via the network in a zone via its 
\family typewriter
<nickname>.vm
\family default
 name (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "VM-DNS"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 for more details).
\end_layout

