\begin_layout Subsection
Threat model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
PADSI is meant to address the following threats:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
system compromise via an application opening a malicious specially crafted document (Web browser,
 PDF reader,
 ...) which leads to the local execution of a malicious program or piece of code by the threat actor;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
system compromise via clicking on a malicious link from a Web browser which leads to social engineering attacks where the user is lead to provide credentials or other sensitive information.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Specifically,
 PADSI aims at reducing the impacts of a compromise of the system:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
on the system itself by protecting access to files and other sensitive information (like credentials exfiltration via phishing and files exfiltration and loss via ransomware);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
on the other elements of the infrastructure to which the system is connected to (via network access limitations and monitoring).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Use cases
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
PADSI is adapted to the following use cases:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
enterprise usage without any strong adherence to Windows,
 to protect access to company data and allow secure activities;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
development activities to (partially) address the risk of supply chain attacks;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
personal usage to protect one's personal information.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Architecture
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This sections provides an architectural view of how PADSI is build and operates,
 and is meant to be used by people in need to understand how PADSI works under the hood (administrators,
 security auditors,
 developers,
 etc).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
PADSI services
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "PADSI services"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Systemd service
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
PADSI is run via the 
\series bold
PADSI systemd service
\series default
,
 as root which actually runs the 
\family typewriter
padsi-system-service
\family default
 program.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To handle updates to the PADSI software itself after a new version of a package has been installed,
 the 
\family typewriter
/run/padsi-needrestart
\family default
 file is created.
 When no user is connected,
 then PADSI's systemd service is restarted (after a check to make sure the deployed configuration,
 which may have been changed,
 is still valid).
 When the PADSI's systemd service starts,
 it removes any 
\family typewriter
/run/padsi-needrestart
\family default
 file which may still be present.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to customize when the PADSI service is allowed to restart,
 the configuration can specify a program to execute before the restart occurs.
 If this program's exit status is not zero,
 then the restart will not be performed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Note
\bar default
s:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
this optional process is executed as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

padsi
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 user and not root.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the restart is triggered by sending the TERM signal to itself,
 which cause it to stop nicely and systemd is the one which actually restarts the service.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
User service
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When a user opens a desktop session,
 the system service spawns a 
\series bold
PADSI user service
\series default
 (the 
\family typewriter
user-service
\family default
 process) as the user which will be responsible to set up any zone or other artifact as required by the user and depending on the configuration.
 This user service is then killed when the user logs out.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The user service communicates with the system service via dedicated pipes to ask it to execute privileged actions such as mounting drives or modifying the network configuration.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The user service in turn sets up a small REST server which listens on a Unix socket protected so that only the user can access it (a user can't access another user's dedicated similar socket).
 All the actions a user might want to do (start processes,
 VMs,
 ...) are performed via HTTP
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
No HTTPS,
 access control is ensured by the system's filesystem permissions and there is little way to intercept communications taking place through Unix sockets.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 calls to the user service's REST service via the 
\family typewriter
padsi-cli
\family default
 command line tool (or any other tool which can use HTTP).
 Note that the user service's REST API is only accessible from the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 namespaces,
 but not from any program running in a zone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following diagram illustrates this point of architecture where two users (one with UID XXX and the other with UID YYY) have simultaneously opened a graphical session:
\end_layout

