Host network restriction
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "host-network-access"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Many processes will run outside of any zone (all the OS processes,
 some processes which are configured to be executed as such,
 like the calculator,
 and maybe some processes the used managed to execute outside of any zone
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Allowing the user to do so is not a security risk but will merely be a UX degradation.
\end_layout

\end_inset

) even when the PADSI system is enforced.
 These processes' network capabilities are restricted based on PADSI's global configuration (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "global-configuration"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) using the system's netfilter firewall.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that some network access will automatically be allowed:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
DHCP requests and responses so the automatic network configuration of the physical (wired or wireless) network interfaces is still operational;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
DNS requests and responses up to when their are not needed anymore,
 to enable the usage of VPNs which endpoint is defined as a FQDN and not an IP address;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
access to VPN servers;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
communications from all the zones' network infrastructure (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "zone-infra"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) and the established VPNs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The system administrator may also allow for example incoming SSH connections (preferably on a randomly chosen port or with port knocking and using SSH keys) to still be able to remotely log in as a fallback mechanism.
\end_layout

