\begin_layout Subsubsection
DNS & Firewall services
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "Zone DNS-&-Firewall"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As stated above,
 each zone uses the DNS services of the 
\series bold
zone's network infrastructure
\series default
 actually provided by an instance of the 
\family typewriter
unbound
\family default
 DNS server.
 The DNS server and the firewall in that 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bubble
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 cooperate to enforce the network restrictions defined in the zone's configuration.
 Namely:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
all the rules based on IP addresses (and optionally protocol type and port numbers) are directly loaded into the firewall – the communications of applications using IP addresses without DNS resolution are thus directly filtered by the firewall;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
any DNS request from an application is handled by the DNS server which may:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
reject it;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
resolve it itself;
 or
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
forward its resolution to a DNS resolver as configured in the 
\family typewriter
/etc/resolv.conf
\family default
 file present in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bubble
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
if the DNS resolution is successful,
 then the DNS server communicates the actual resolved IPs and the associated TTL to the firewall which will allow them up to when the TTL expires (plus some extra time to allow for long running applications which reuse previously obtained responses without taking care of the TTL);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the DNS resolution can be forwarded to an external resolver,
 or be directly handled by the 
\family typewriter
unbound
\family default
 DNS server itself.
\end_layout

