\begin_layout Subsection
Hotplug events
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "Hotplug-netlink"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Many programs need to be notified of hardware hotplug events:
 Web browsers for FIDO2 authenticators,
 the VM viewer to let the user 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pass
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 the plugged devices to the VM,
 etc.
 Several methods can be used to be notified,
 the most common for programs is to open a 
\series bold
netlink
\series default
 socket with the 
\family typewriter
NETLINK_KOBJECT_UEVENT
\family default
 protocol.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Netlink sockets are bound to the network namespace,
 which means that a program running in a non 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 network namespace will not receive any event from the Linux kernel.
 To circumvent this limitation,
 when needed,
 the following setup is instanciated:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a shared 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

shim
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 library is preloaded to intercept any call to the 
\family typewriter
socket()
\family default
 system call.
 If the call is for a netlink socket with the hotplug events protocol,
 then it asks a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

host helper
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 process to actually open the socket;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

netlink proxy
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 program (running in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 network namespace) serves requests from the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

shim
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 via a Unix socket,
 opens the netlink socket and pass it back to the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

shim
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 process.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename netlink.png
	width 100col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Mounting filesystems (using FUSE)
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "FUSE"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If allowed by the zone configuration,
 normal users are allowed to mount filesystems using the FUSE technology (this is used by XDG portals or applications like Borg backup).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Applications using FUSE rely 
\shape italic
in-fine
\shape default
 on the 
\family typewriter
/usr/bin/fusermount
\family default
 program which is setuid to obtain the necessary privileges and the 
\family typewriter
/dev/fuse
\family default
 device file.
 For security purposes,
 the 
\family typewriter
bwrap
\family default
 tool drops the capability to ever elevate privileges again,
 which render the usage of setuid programs useless,
 which is the reason PADSI sets up a dedicated infrastructure for FUSE mounting (and unmounting) filesystems:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename FUSE.png
	width 50col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From a technical point of view:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
unmounting FUSE mounted filesystems similarly calls the 
\family typewriter
umount
\family default
 program which is also setuid;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
PADSI sets up a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

mount-server
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (per-zone and run as the user) in charge of executing the privileged 
\family typewriter
fusermount
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
umount
\family default
 programs (actual privileges are thus handled by the Linux OS's configuration) which listens on a Unix socket
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Using a Unix socket is required to pass file descriptors between the FUSE clients and the actual 
\family typewriter
fusermount
\family default
 programs.
\end_layout

\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the 
\family typewriter
/usr/bin/fusermount
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
/usr/bin/umount
\family default
 files are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

masked
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 by PADSI's associated 
\family typewriter
fusermount
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
umount
\family default
 proxies to relay calls to the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

mount-server
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 process,
 via the 
\family typewriter
/bubble/run/padsi-fuse.sock
\family default
 Unix socket;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the unmount operations can be performed by the user,
 but also by the PADSI user service when the user logs out.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Limitations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As the actual mount operation is performed by the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

mount-server
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 the mount point must be visible to it,
 which limits to 
\family typewriter
/bubble/run
\family default
 and its sub-directories or the user's home directory.
 Other restrictions may also apply depending on the type of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

FUSE engine
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 used.
\end_layout

