\begin_layout Subsubsection
Outlined windows
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The colored outline of all windows for programs running in a zone is actually applied by a GNOME shell extension (similar extensions could probably be developed for other desktop environments).
 Specifically,
 the extension matches the mount and network namespaces of the zone to the colors defined for that zone and display the outline.
 The user can disable the extension,
 but he will then not be able to visually know which window correspond to a program executed in which zone (all the other isolation mechanisms are not impacted).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Specifically,
 when PADSI starts a new zone to execute a program for the benefit of a user,
 it employs the following steps:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
create a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bubble
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for the zone,
 which automatically creates new namespaces;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
write the actual new namespaces' references to the 
\family typewriter
/run/padsi/zone-infos/colors.json
\family default

\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Generated by the PADSI system service.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 file for the GNOME Shell extension to (re)load;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
start programs which will present windows to the end user.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To determine the color of the outline to apply to a given window,
 the PADSI GNOME shell extension then:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
gets the PID of the window for which the function is called;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
gets the mount and network namespaces of that PID;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
uses the contents of the 
\family typewriter
/run/padsi/zone-infos/colors.json
\family default
 file to get the color to apply.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Outlined icons
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When an application is started,
 Mutter (the GNOME Wayland compositor) determines the APP ID of the application
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Using some quite complex heuristics relying on the APP ID specified by the application,
 if any,
 or the APP ID specified by Flatpak (via the 
\family typewriter
.flatpak-info
\family default
 file located at the root of the sandbox in which the application is started) or by Snap (via the 
\family typewriter
/proc/<pid>/attr/current
\family default
 information).
\end_layout

\end_inset

 which is then used by the GNOME shell to assign a desktop entry which name is 
\family typewriter
<APP ID>.desktop
\family default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Pending any modification of Mutter to better integrate PADSI's features,
 Mutter is actually 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

patched
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 while running by hooking the functions which handle APP ID retrieval using 
\family typewriter
LD_PRELOAD
\family default
.
 When one of the hooked function is called,
 PADSI:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
gets the PID of the window for which the function is called;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
gets the mount and network namespaces of that PID;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
use the contents of the 
\family typewriter
/run/padsi/zones-infos/prefixes.txt
\family default

\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Also generated by the PADSI system service when it generates the 
\family typewriter
/run/padsi/zone-infos/colors.json
\family default
 file.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 file to determine the zone's prefix (in the 
\family typewriter
padsi.<zone name>
\family default
 format);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
concatenates the real APP ID (as obtained using the real and unhooked Mutter's functions) with that prefix and keeps a cache of it;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
returns this 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

adapted
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 APP ID.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Clipboard sharing restrictions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sharing the content of the clipboard between applications in different zones can lead to data exfiltration risks where an application in a zone can request any content copied by the user from another application in another zone (and copy it to disk or exfiltrate it via the network,
 think for example about passwords copied from a password manager).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to address this issue,
 PADSI allows to define clipboard sharing restrictions between zones.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When enabled,
 and as necessary,
 any program in any zone communicates with the Wayland compositor via a dedicated 
\series bold
per-zone Wayland proxy
\series default
 which filters copy/paste related messages according to the configuration.
 Specifically,
 the wayland proxy:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
sits between all the UI applications in the associated zone,
 filters all the messages,
 and deeply analyses the messages related to copy/paste;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
when a copy offer is sent by a Wayland client (in any zone),
 the wayland proxy adds the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\family typewriter
padsi/zone;z=<zone name>
\family default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 mime type to the list of mime types the client sends;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
when a copy offer is received by a wayland proxy,
 it retreives the zone in which the client is from the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\family typewriter
padsi/zone;z=<zone name>
\family default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 mime type and allows or blocks the copy offer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\color red
IMPORTANT NOTE
\series default
\color inherit
:
 the clipboard sharing restrictions don't apply to contents copied and pasted via the X11 mechanisms,
 so X11 should be disabled accordingly to avoid any policy bypass.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
X11 support
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Even though X11 has been superseded by Wayland,
 in some cases it might be necessary to run X11 applications.
 so,
 if a zone configuration requires it,
 access to the X11 server is provided from within the zone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\color red
IMPORTANT NOTE
\series default
\color inherit
:
 any X11 application in a zone can access to the events of any X11 application in any other zone:
 
\bar under
this breaks the zone's isolation features
\bar default
.
 As such,
 one should be very careful how X11 access is provided in zones.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Desktop features
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Desktop features like screen sharing or notifications are provided via the D-Bus session bus which is not accessible by the zones where programs are executed.
 When those features are required,
 the zone instantiates a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

D-Bus router
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 which acts as the D-Bus session bus and actually forwards the messages to and from the applications and the real D-Bus session bus based on the features requested.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For screen sharing,
 refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "Annex: Screen-sharing"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
D-Bus router
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "D-Bus router"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For better isolation,
 each zone has its own D-Bus bus and no access to the system D-Bus,
 which means application can't access features provided by the real D-Bus session bus such as screen sharing or deaktop notifications.
 A zone can nonetheless have some options if thoses features are required,
 and in this case,
 a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

D-Bus router
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is also instantiated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The D-Bus router itself can communicate with both the zone's D-Bus bus and the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

real
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 desktop's D-Bus session bus and presents itself as the D-Bus session bus.
 It then
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
forwards messages from the application to the correct bus depending on the features required;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
forwards messages from the busses to the application.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename dbus-router-overview.png
	width 60col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To do so,
 the D-Bus router:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
uses matching rules on messages (based on the service,
 interface,
 or objects);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
analyses messages' contents to extract relevant information (such as connection IDs);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

masquerade
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 destination addresses;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
guarantees that messages from a remote D-Bus server are always forwarded to the client D-Bus application in the same order in which they arrived;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
TODO:
 handle the case where 2 apps on the 2 busses have the same address :x.y
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the application has no way to bypass the D-Bus router and directly connect to the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

real
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 D-Bus session bus
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
if the need arise,
 another D-Bus router could be spawned to create a proxy to the D-Bus system bus,
 or the 
\family typewriter
xdg-dbus-proxy
\family default
 program could also be used for that.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Security devices integration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Zones can set to allow users to use smartcards and/or FIDO2 authenticators.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Smartcards
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "smartcard"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Smartcard access is provided to applications using the PCSC daemon (from the OpenSC project),
 zones are primarily given access via its Unix socket (
\family typewriter
/run/pcscd/pcscd.comm
\family default
).
 Web browsers' policies are adapted to enable direct usage of the smartcard without any further configuration.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
on Debian systems (maybe others),
 beyond the traditional Unix permissions on the socket file the PCSC daemon running in the system uses PolKit to determine if a process is allowed access,
 using the DBus session bus.
 Because zones set up their own DBus session bus,
 the PolKit permissions must be adapted as necessary via the creation of a 
\family typewriter
/etc/polkit-1/rules.d/00-padsi-user-<UID>.rules
\family default
 file (which is taken into account as soon as created and removed when the user logs out):
 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "basicstyle={\footnotesize\ttfamily}"
inline false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

polkit.addRule(function(action,
 subject) {
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    if ((action.id=="org.debian.pcsc-lite.access_pcsc" || 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

		 action.id=="org.debian.pcsc-lite.access_card") &&
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

		 subject.user=="<USERNAME>") {
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

        return polkit.Result.YES;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    }
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

});
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
direct access to devices in 
\family typewriter
/dev/bus/XXX
\family default
 is not granted,
 as no programs requiring it has been identified.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that the full path to the PKCS#11 library file must be provided when this feature is configured (which means that different zones can use different PKCS#11 drivers,
 which may help solve PKCS#11 drivers incompatibilities).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Applications in zones will be granted access to smartcards if the 
\family typewriter

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

PKCS11
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\family default
 option is enabled for the zone (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "zones-configuration"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)
\end_layout

