\begin_layout Subsubsection
Web proxy's architecture
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The proxy's architecture is as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename web-proxy.png
	width 90col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Notes
\bar default
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the Web proxy also proxies Web traffic which is allowed directly by the zone's configuration to simplify applications' configuration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the default policy is to block accesses;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
from a technical point of view,
 if all traffic is routed to Web proxies,
 DNS resolution can be disabled as it's not used.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Web redirection's architecture
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "Web-redirection"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Web redirection is a purely cosmetic feature which has no security value but is a UX bonus when a user tries to connect to a Web site (either directly or having clicked on a link) which is blocked in the zone where the Web browser used is executed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Without this feature,
 the DNS resolution in such case will fail and the browser will display an error message.
 However,
 when this feature is enabled for that zone:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the browser displays a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

site blocked notice
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 with some informational description;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
depending on the settings and if the blocked can be opened using a Web browser in another zone,
 then a notification will be presented in the GNOME desktop along with an action button to actually open the Web site in the other zone(s).
 The user then only has to click a button to open the requested Web page in the same browser but in a suitable zone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Limitations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Web redirection has several limitations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
only HTTP GET requests are taken into account;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the feature is enabled only if:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
the path and query of the requested resource is less than 100 bytes (to avoid potential data exfiltration);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the connection port is among a common list (80,
 443,
 8080,
 8443);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the requested page has a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

referer
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 header (to filter out non user initiated requests);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the requested web site is not among the AdBlocked list.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Paragraph
Architecture
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Displaying a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

site blocked notice
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for any Web site,
 and without any Web browser security warning for those accessed via HTTPS requires:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
redirection Web server
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 serving the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
site blocked notice
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename web-redirection-blocked.png
	width 60col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
that the DNS server for the zone resolve any non allowed request to that fallback server;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
that the web redirection server generates a certificate adapted to the requested host using a dedicated certification authority (named 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Web access denied CA
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
that this CA be trusted by the Web browser in the zone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Furthermore,
 actually notifying that the Web site can be reached using the same Web browser but from another zone requires a 
\series bold
notification service
\series default
 to:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
ask the GNOME desktop to display a notification (via a DBus message):
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename web-redirection-notif.png
	width 40col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
ask the PADSI user service (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "PADSI services"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) to actually start the Web browser in the selected zone and open the blocked URL.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The architecture is the following one:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename web-redirection-archi.png
	width 60col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Notes
\bar default
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Web access denied CA
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
is short lived and per zone:
 its private key and self signed root certificate are created when the zone's infrastructure is started and removed when the zone is destroyed;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
is only declared as trusted in the Web browsers (via specific policies) but not in the zone's 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

system
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 files;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
is generated when the zone is started,
 stored as a PKCS#12 file (using a random password),
 passed to the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

web-infra
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 application which uses it.
 Once this setup phase is done,
 the password is lost making sure no malicious actor can use that CA for nefarious purposes.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
this is not a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

middle box
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as the web redirection server will only ever display a notice as a static HTML Web page and not itself query the actual Web server before inspecting and eventually giving it to the Web browser:
 there is no SSL/TLS 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

decryption
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and inspection;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
this feature is implemented in a way such that a malicious threat actor:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
can't bypass network restrictions and communicate in any way with his command and control services without any user interaction;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
can't get any information about PADSI's actual configuration.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
the implementation of this feature using Web browser extensions did not work out as expected because Google's manifest V3 which Chrome uses does not allow for that kind of extension to built.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Flatpak applications
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Flatpak applications are executed as any other application from a running zone,
 creating their own sandbox using the BubbleWrap tool.
 There is no specific treatment for Flatpak applications.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Virtual machines
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
PADSI allows end users to run virtual machines in the context of a zone:
 each VM is run as a 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "QEMU"
target "https://www.qemu.org"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 process and not via libvirt or other kind of VM management.
 End users are not however by default allowed to install themselves virtual machines,
 this role is dedicated to system administrators which:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
install a VM from traditional boot medias provided by the selected OS's manufacturer;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
import that newly created VM (via its disk image and UEFI firmware files) to create a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
base
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 VM (a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

base
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 VM can also be saved on a PADSI system and loaded into another one);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
update the VM when deemed necessary (for instance to apply security patches or modify the VM's configuration);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
make that updated 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

base
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 VM available to users.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Virtual machines are defined globally and made available on a per zone basis.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
About VM versions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As introduced in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "VM-version"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 PADSI imposes a lifecycle over the VM versions associated to a VM definition;
 this process heavily relies on 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "QEMU snapshots"
target "https://wiki.qemu.org/Documentation/CreateSnapshot"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 As a reminder,
 a VM version is a set of coherent files used to run a specific version of a VM:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a VM disk image file (with the 
\family typewriter
.img
\family default
 extension);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a VM's UEFI firmware 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "OVMF"
target "https://github.com/tianocore/tianocore.github.io/wiki/OVMF"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 file (with the 
\family typewriter
.vars
\family default
 extension);
 and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a VM's information file (with the 
\family typewriter
.infos
\family default
 extension) which,
 among other things,
 keeps track of the life of that VM version.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A given VM version can be in several 
\series bold
usage states
\series default
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
CREATED
\series default
:
 the associated files have just been created and have never been used;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
STOPPED
\series default
:
 the VM is currently not running;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
RUNNING
\series default
:
 the VM is currently running;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
DISCARDED
\series default
:
 the VM was marked as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

to be destroyed
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and the associated files will probably soon be removed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Also,
 VM versions are associated to 
\series bold
lifecycle types
\series default
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
BASE
\series default
:
 a VM version which is actually never directly used to run a VM but serves as a reference,
 making it 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

read-only
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 This is what is deployed by system administrators.
 A base version is not associated to any zone or any end user.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
USER
\series default
:
 a VM version which has been customized for a specific user (user name,
 ID,
 etc.).
 A user VM version is always a snapshot of a BASE VM version and also must be considered as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

read-only
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as,
 once created,
 it is never directly used to run a VM.
 A user version is always associated to a zone (i.e.
 the customization processus will occur once per zone and per base VM version) and an end user.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
SNAPSHOT
\series default
:
 a VM version which is actually run by the end user.
 A snapshot VM version is always a snapshot of a user VM version and can be given a nickname as the end user is allowed to run several snapshot VMs at the same time.
 A snapshot version is also always associated to a zone and an end user.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To make sure end users can run VMs while at the same time an administrator can update the base VM,
 PADSI introduces the notion of a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
staging area
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 which is where installation and updates occur before being 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
published
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and declared usable by the end user.
 The staging area is not associated to any zone,
 but is associated to an end user.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following sections detail the relations between the different VM versions during different usage scenarios.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Privileges management
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each virtual machine configuration,
 the system administrator has to define the following privileges regarding the VM's usage (which differs with the actual privileges the user may have inside the guest OS of the VM):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the 
\series bold
privilege to install
\series default
 the VM allows specified users to boot the VM from an ISO image and actually install the VM creating a VM version in the staged area.
 To actually be used,
 that new VM version will have to be published;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the 
\series bold
privilege to update
\series default
 the VM allows specified users to create a new VM version in the staged area,
 and run the VM based on it to make changes to the guest OS such as applying patches,
 install or remove software,
 or change its configuration.
 This privilege also allows the user to publish the VM version to make it available to users who can actually run the VM;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the 
\series bold
privilege to run
\series default
 the VM allows specified users to create one or more snapshot VM versions of a published VM version and run them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
QEMU snapshots
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To avoid duplicating VM images files (the files which represents the virtual HDD or the VM and can reach several Gb),
 PADSI uses QEMU's snapshots feature which allow a VM image file to store only the changes from a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

backend
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 VM image file.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This means VM versions usually have a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

dependency
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or a cascade of dependencies over other VM versions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As an example,
 for a Windows VM:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the BASE (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

real
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) VM image file size will range from 8Gb to 20Gb;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the USER VM image file size will be around 200Mb.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And for a Linux VM:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the BASE VM image file size will range from 3b to 10Gb;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the USER VM image size will be around 15Mb.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
VM version installation / import
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
VM versions can be installed from an OS's manufacturer ISO image (like any virtual machine can be installed) or imported from an existing VM disk image (a VM version also be loaded from an archive of an already existing VM version possibly on another PADSI system,
 refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "VM-save-load"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The installation workflow is as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The installation process creates a staged VM version (replacing any previous one unless that VM version was not marked as discarded);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If deemed Ok by the administrator,
 the VM version is then published to create a base VM version.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The import workflow is more direct as the import process directly creates a base VM version.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename VM lifecycle1.png
	width 50col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
VM version run
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When a user wants to run a new VM,
 PADSI will automatically create a user VM version (based on the last base VM version available) if there is not yet one,
 and only then create a snapshot VM version to actually run the VM on.
 If the user VM version for that user already exists,
 then a snapshot VM version will simply be created from it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename VM lifecycle2.png
	width 60col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
VM version update
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To update an existing VM version,
 PADSI simply creates a new staged VM version based on the last available base VM version and run the VM with this VM version.
 Once the administrator is satisfied with the updated VM version,
 he can publish it as a new base VM version:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename VM lifecycle3.png
	width 35col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that the new BASE VM version ends up being a snapshot of the previous base VM version.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
VM version save and load
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "VM-save-load"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
Loading
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 a VM refers to preparing to use a VM version which has been 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
saved
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 by an instance of PADSI,
 as opposed to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
importing
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 a VM's disk image which may have been created by any tool.
 The difference is that when loading a VM version,
 the VM version's other files are also taken into account,
 and that disk images' dependencies are also handled.
 Saving a VM version to load it later on a possibly different PADSI instance generates a TAR archive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each archive has the following contents:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the 
\family typewriter
manifest.json
\family default
 manifest file containing various information about the contents of the archive;
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
one or more 
\family typewriter
vm-version-<index>
\family default
 directories which each contain all the files making a specific VM version (
\family typewriter
base.img
\family default
,
 
\family typewriter
base.vars
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
base.infos
\family default
),
 where:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
index=0
\family default
 for the VM version being saved
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
index=1
\family default
 for the VM version on which the VM version being saved depend (its image file being a snapshot of this VM version);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
index=X+1
\family default
 for the VM version on which the VM version of index=X depends.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
If the PADSI instance where the archive will be loaded already has a specific VM version,
 then the archive may contain only a reference of that VM version (as the SHA256 hash of the VM image file) and not include any subsequent dependencies.
 In that case,
 the hash of the VM version is included in the manifest in the 
\family typewriter
dependency-hash
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
dependency-size
\family default
 attributes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example,
 in the following diagram:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the PADSI instance where the archive is loaded already contains a VM version X;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the archive will load a VM version X+1 based on the VM version X;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
it will also load the VM version X+2 based on the VM version X+1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename VM lifecycle5.png
	width 45col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that PADSI will try to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

reuse
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 immutable VM disk images as much as possible to avoid clutter (before each VM disk image is loaded,
 its file size and possibly hash are compared to what's already present in the system).
 This means that actual VM version numbers may differ between the system where the VM versions were saved and the system where they are loaded after an archive has been loaded.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
VM versions merge and cleanups
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because of the way PADSI uses snapshots during the lifetime of VM versions,
 there can be quite long string of actual QEMU image files linked together associated to base VM versions.
 At some point though,
 the snapshot VM versions will all be snapshots of user VM versions based on the latest base VM version,
 with some user VM versions having become useless.
 To avoid clutter,
 PADSI then proposes to:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
remove discarded snapshot VM versions;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
remove unused (non referenced by any snapshot VM version) user VM versions;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
merge base VM versions with the base VM version of which it is a snapshot.
 Note that this operation is only possible when no other VM version references the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

lower
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 VM version to merge into.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename VM lifecycle4.png
	width 35col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Relation to the zone' network infrastructure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The QEMU process making the VM 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

run
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is not directly executed in the zone's 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bubble
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 along with other programs:
 it runs in its own 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bubble
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 along with the ancillary services to support the VM such as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a DNS service and the associated firewall which functions as for the zone's infrastructure (refer to §
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "Zone DNS-&-Firewall"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) which allows the administrators to restrict the communications capabilities of the VM even further than for the associated zone;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a DHCP service to configure the IP address,
 gateway and DNS resolver of the VM's;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
one or more 
\family typewriter
virtiofsd
\family default
 services which provide each the capability to access files from the zone (more section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "VM Files-sharing"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a VM viewer which is the actual UI through which the end user can use the VM;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a VM monitor which is in charge of detecting when the QEMU or VM viewer processes terminate to end the VM's 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

session
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 properly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From a network standpoint,
 the VM is run with a single Ethernet network interface via which it can communicate with the programs and other VM in the same zone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following diagram illustrates the situation when a VM is running,
 note that:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the VM's IP address 
\bar under
always
\bar default
 being 
\family typewriter
192.168.244.2/24
\family default
 while the corresponding IP address of the associated 
\family typewriter
tapvm
\family default
 network interface in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bubble
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is 
\family typewriter
192.168.244.1/24
\family default
;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bubble
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

's own interface to communicate with the zone's network infrastructure is in the same sub network as the other 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bubbles
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in the same zone (
\bar under
always
\bar default
 in the 
\family typewriter
192.168.0.0/16
\family default
 address range);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the VM viewer uses a Unix network socket to communicate via the SPICE protocol with the VM (no other program can 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

grab
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 the VM's display because the Unix socket file is visible only within the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bubble
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename VM network infra.png
	width 75col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the zone uses Web proxies (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "Web-infra"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 then PADSI also runs a dedicated Web proxy for the VM,
 which
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
blocks requests to Web services which are denied;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
forwards all other requests to the web proxy running the zone's network infrastructure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Notes
\bar default
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the WPAD feature is available;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the Web redirection feature,
 if enabled in the zone is not enabled for the VM as it makes little sense to do so.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Relation to zones
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The PADSI's administrator defines which VM can be run in the context of each zone,
 and by with end user.
 This is fine but installing or updating a VM requires different network accesses,
 specifically resources available only via Internet and these resources may not be available from the zone(s) where the VM can be run.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To cope with this situation,
 VM installation and update are not done in the context of a predefined zone but (unless otherwise specified),
 PADSI will determine which zone is best suited to be used and run the VM in that zone but without any zone's files being accessible (in other words:
 benefit from the zone's network infrastructure,
 but don't let the VM use the zone's files).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
VM viewer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While a virtual machine is running,
 the associated VM viewer process is kept running,
 even if it does not display any window.
 When the VM terminates (either properly shut down or if the QEMU process is killed one way or another),
 PADSI marks the VM as discarded and remove any associated resources.
 However,
 the VM viewer is not shown by default if VM has its 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

show-ui
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 attribute set to 
\family typewriter
False
\family default
,
 but it can still be shown afterwards (sending 
\family typewriter
USR1
\family default
 or using the PADSI tools).
 Specifically:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When the VM is being installed or updated,
 the VM viewer is always show in console mode when the VM is being installed or updated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When the VM is customized for the user:
 the VM viewer,
 if shown,
 will only display the startup window while the VM is being customized for the user.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When the VM is actuall run:
 when the VM boots,
 the VM viewer will display a startup window to hide the VM's actual console to the user while still alloing him to show the console or discard the VM.
 If the use has not opted to show the console,
 it will be shown when the user session in the VM has been opened and the VM is thus completely usable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
SSH connections
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because SSH is such a powerful tool,
 when a zone in which VMs can be running is started,
 a new pair of fresh SSH keys is created with the aim to be used to SSH into all the VMs in that zone.
 Then,
 when a VM is actually started:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the SSH private key is copied to the zone's 
\family typewriter
~/.ssh/padsi-vm-key
\family default
 file;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
via the PADSI agent (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "VM agent"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
the zone's 
\family typewriter
~/.ssh/config
\family default
 file is modified to create an entry for the VM;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the SSH's public key is copied to the 
\family typewriter
etc/padsi-vm-key.pub
\family default
 file and ultimately appended to the 
\family typewriter
~/.ssh/authorized_keys
\family default
 file in the user's HOME directory in the VM;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
the VM's SSH's public key is scanned and:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
the public SSH key added to the zone's 
\family typewriter
~/.ssh/known_hosts
\family default
 file (one entry per domain name for the VM in the zone,
 refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "VM-DNS"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a Host entry is created in the zone's 
\family typewriter
~/.ssh/config
\family default
 file
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
so that the use can simply run ssh to get a shell in the VM (of course if the VM offers an SSH service).
 Note that any information in those files which may have been added by the user is kept AS-IS.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsubsection
USB redirection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
USB devices redirection in the context of virtual machines is performed by the VM viewer using the Spice technology which mainly requires:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
to be informed of devices hotplug events and be able to access devices (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "Hotplug-netlink"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

);
 and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
to be able to execute the 
\family typewriter
spice-client-glib-usb-acl-helper
\family default
 helper program to change the ACL of devices files.
 This helper program is setuid to set the correct ACL,
 but the setuid possibility is lost when running within a BubbleWrap context.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 this second point is performed via a dedicated component made of two parts:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

helper proxy
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 which is executed by the Spice component of VM viewer thinking it actually is the 
\family typewriter
spice-client-glib-usb-acl-helper
\family default
 program,
 and which behaves exactly like it from its point of view;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the usb redirection server which is running in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 namespaces which is in charge of executing the actual 
\family typewriter
spice-client-glib-usb-acl-helper
\family default
 program (which can elevate its privileges by being setuid).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All the while,
 once this setup is done,
 the Spice component of the VM viewer can directly communicate with the actual USB ACL helper as this process uses the same stdin,
 stdout and stderr file descriptors as the ones defined by the VM viewer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following schema summarizes this setup:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename USB-redirection.png
	width 90col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Safe resources usage
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When a USB device is being used by a virtual machine,
 the host system and other virtual machines lose their ability to use that device.
 This is even more salient in the case of smartcards where the host's PCSC daemon (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "smartcard"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) will prevent any virtual machine from acquiring the usage of the smartcard.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to tackle this problem,
 the PADSI system service maintains a list of which USB device is used by which virtual machine (or by the host in case of smartcards) from information received by the 
\series bold
USB devices monitor
\series default

\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Which technically uses an eBPF program as traditional inotify and fanotify mechanisms don't work for devtmpfs.
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 and allows the viewer of each virtual machine to query this status and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

ask
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 before redirecting a USB device.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename USB-redirection-devices.png
	width 100col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Note
\bar default
:
 the PCSCD service is automatically started and and stopped by PADSI when a VM wants to use a smartcard.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
PADSI VM agent
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "VM agent"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\series bold
PADSI VM agent
\series default
 is a small component via which PADSI interacts with running VMs mainly to customize the VM for a specific end user and mount the shared directories into the VM,
 and run specific programs on boot.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 the PADSI agent can also implements a REST API to:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
get the status of the system;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
execute programs;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
shutdown the system (used by the VM viewer).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Technically,
 the PADSI agent is given access to resources shared by the host (mounted in 
\family typewriter
/run/padsi-agent/
\family default
 for a Linux guest OS and in the 
\family typewriter
Z:
\backslash

\family default
 directory for a Windows guest OS):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
information about the context in which the VM is run (in the 
\family typewriter
etc/config.*
\family default
 files);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the list of files shared with the host and the associated mount point 
\bar under
in the user's home directory or profile
\bar default
,
 (in the 
\family typewriter
etc/mountpoints.*
\family default
 files);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the program to execute at boot time (the 
\family typewriter
bin/on-boot.<ext>
\family default
 file) determined from the VM's configuration and usage context (update,
 user customization or simply run by the end user);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
some support scripts (in the 
\family typewriter
lib/
\family default
 directory);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
if the user has set up a 
\family typewriter
~/.padsi-agent
\family default
 in the zone where the VM is run,
 then that directory is also mounted in the 
\family typewriter
user/
\family default
 directory along with 
\family typewriter
etc/
\family default
,
 
\family typewriter
bin/
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
lib/
\family default
 directories.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following diagram illustrates this:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename VM management.png
	width 90col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The PADSI agent is provided as a package which must be installed in the VM (in the installation or in a update phase) and is implemented as an OS level service.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Other SSH keys
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the user already has some SSH public keys as 
\family typewriter
~/.padsi-agent/.ssh/*.pub
\family default
 files in the zone where the VM is run,
 then those keys are also appended to the the 
\family typewriter
~/.ssh/authorized_keys
\family default
 file in the user's HOME directory in the VM.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Miscellaneous
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
User configuration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As each VM is customized for a specific user before being run and because that VM can only be used by the user for which it was customized,
 PADSI configures the created user in a very 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

loose
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 way from traditional user configuration:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the user has no password;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the session is automatically opened for the user (graphical or textual).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Files sharing
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "VM Files-sharing"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Files sharing is actually 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

directory
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 sharing as a VM configuration cannot reference sharing of a single file.
 Additionally,
 all files are shared as the UID of the user running the VM.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As stated above,
 all the directories shared by configuration between the host and the VM are mounted relative to the user's home directory (or Windows profile);
 no user controlled file can be mounted outside.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,if it exists,
 the 
\family typewriter
~/.padsi-agent
\family default
 directory exists in the host's home directory of the considered zone,
 it is mounted read-only in the 
\family typewriter
user/
\family default
 directory relative to the resources directory where PADSI sets up resources for the VM,
 namely 
\family typewriter
/run/padsi-agent/user/
\family default
 for a Linux guest OS and 
\family typewriter
Z:
\backslash
user
\family default
 for a windows guest OS).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From a technical point of view:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
for a Linux guest OS,
 the shared files are simply mounted as the expected target directory after having created its parent directory if necessary;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
for a Windows guest OS:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
the shared directories are mounted as drive letters starting at 
\family typewriter
Z:

\family default
 and going backwards for each shared directory;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the target directory is removed if it existed,
 and its parent directory created if necessary;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the target directory is a link to the drive letter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
all the drive letters mounted as such are hidden to improve UX.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
TODO:
 printing
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Bubbles
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 implementation
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "nsbubble"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Many components in PADSI are isolated from one another using what is referred to as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bubbles
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 This section describe what a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bubble
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is and its architecture.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bubble
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is a set of processes isolated from the rest of the system using kernel namespaces.
 At its core a bubble is set up and governed by the 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Bubblewrap"
target "https://github.com/containers/bubblewrap"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 tool (hence the name).
 Every 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bubble
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 has a dedicated 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 process (the 
\family typewriter
init
\family default
 program) which:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
is the first process started;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
acts as an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 process in the way it inherits parenthood for all the orphaned processes in the bubble;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
offers a REST API available via a Unix socket (thus benefiting from the Linux's files permissions to control its access) to manage processes running within the bubble from outside the bubble;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
can restart processes marked as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

restart
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 if they terminate (up to a pre-defined rate to avoid overload due to a buggy process);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
can kill all the processes running in the bubble if some process marked as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

required
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 ever terminates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following diagram illustrates this with a bubble having started two processes:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename nsbubble.png
	width 70col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Command line tools
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "padsi-cli"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
User interactions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Interacting with PADSI can be done using the command line tool 
\family typewriter
padsi-cli
\family default
 which comes as two different flavors:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
one to be used from the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (host) namespaces to perform actions like getting the status,
 or starting programs or virtual machines.
 This tool is not meant to be used directly by the end user but rather by other tools (mainly the GNOME shell to start programs in various zones when the user clicks on icons);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
one to be used from within a zone to perform actions related to managing virtual machines from the command line.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Both versions are installed in the 
\family typewriter
/usr/bin/padsi-cli
\family default
,
 the actual version depends on the execution environment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following diagram shows which program is run depending on the context,
 and to which actual service it connects to:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename cli-variants.png
	width 90col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
For diagnostics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
PADSI administrators or developers can use the 
\family typewriter
padsi-sys
\family default
 tool to obtain or interact with PADSI's running structure,
 like:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
get the network configuration;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
get information about zones and enter specific namespaces.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This tool requires root privileges for most of its operations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
For administrators
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
System administration (be it local or via remote access) requires to execute programs in a specific context (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sys-admin"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 for more details).
 The 
\family typewriter
padsi-do
\family default
 tool can be used to execute programs in that specific context (from local users running 
\family typewriter
sudo
\family default
 to services like SSH for remote access).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Logging
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "Logging"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Being a security product,
 PADSI logs many events which can be later used either to understand bugs or to detect security issues:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
warning and errors events are sent to syslog,
 and presumably handled by the journalctl component depending on the Linux distribution;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
debug and informational events are written to dedicated log files;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The dedicated log files are located in the 
\family typewriter
log/
\family default
 directory below the location of all non volatile PADSI related data (as defined in the configuration and which defaults to 
\family typewriter
/var/padsi/
\family default
).
 Specifically:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
system wide log files are stored in the 
\family typewriter
log/
\family default
 directory;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
per user log files are stored in:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
log/<UID>/
\family default
 for non zone specific events;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
log/<UID>/<zone name>/
\family default
 for zone specific events but not related to any VM;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
log/<UID>/<zone name>/VM-<VM ID>/<VM nickname>/
\family default
 for VM related events in the context of a specific zone.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
administration namespaces (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sys-admin"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) log files are stored in the 
\family typewriter
log/padsi/<admin.NS>/
\family default
 directory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Specifically:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
DNS resolution events located in 
\family typewriter
resolv.log
\family default
 files;
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Note
\bar default
:
 DNS resolution handled locally (for configuration defined and hard coded resolution and for virtual machines' resolution) is not logged;
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
events for network traffic blocked by the firewalls are in 
\family typewriter
firewall.<year>-<month>-<day>.json
\family default
 files;
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Note
\bar default
s:
 non blocked traffic is not logged.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
events generated by the web infrastructure (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "Web-infra"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) are in 
\family typewriter
web-infra.<year>-<month>-<day>.json
\family default
 files;
