\begin_layout Subsubsection
Netfilter logging
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the 
\family typewriter
firewall-logs-group"
\family default
 property in PADSI's global configuration (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "global-configuration"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) specifies a group number,
 then all of PADSI's firewall blocked traffic attempts are logged via NFLOG using the specified group number.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Capturing those events to create the associated JSON files mentionned above is done by the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fw-logger
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 component,
 which acts similarly to the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

ulogd
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 daemon
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Refer to https://www.netfilter.org/projects/ulogd/index.html.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 but is using a hard coded configuration and is spawned in each of the network namespace where netfilter is used
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In its default system-bundled configuration,
 ulogd only gets events generated in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 namespace by netfilter and there is no easy way to have it get similar events from other network namespaces.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
System administration
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sys-admin"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section details the features and corresponding architecture to allow a PADSI system to be administered with regards to the network restrictions which are applied,
 namely remote access and local administration.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Remote access
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Remote access is often used by enterprise administrators or orchestration tools to remotely work on systems or apply policies to them.
 Remote access is usually performed via a network service like SSH.
 In this 
\shape italic
would-be
\shape default
 situation,
 the service is exposed either:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
directly via the system's network configuration,
 but is only readily usable when the system is connected to the enterprise's internal network and not when connected to external unknown networks like home or public Wi-Fi networks (because they all perform some sort of network address translation);
 or
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
via a VPN set up in a way such that all PADSI systems are always connected to a VPN gateway (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "VPN-routing"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) as long as the PADSI system is actually connected to a network through which the VPN gateway can be reached.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

natural
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 approach would be that in the 1st case,
 the network service is running in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 network namespace and a global PADSI configuration which allows the incoming network connection (via the 
\family typewriter
"host-access"
\family default
 key,
 refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "global-configuration"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 In the 2nd case,
 the network service is running in the network namespace where the VPN network interface is.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following schema illustrates this 
\shape italic
would-be
\shape default
 (not the actual,
 and with a WireGuard VPN) setup:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename remote-access-1.png
	width 80col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In both cases,
 the DNS configuration is the one defined by the network the PADSI system is actually connected to
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
PC endpoints' network connectivity is usually configured automatically via DHCP which also contains the DNS configuration to use.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (home,
 public Wi-Fi,
 corporate,
 ...) and often points to a non public resolver (provided by the ISP or the enterprise).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This 
\shape italic
would-be
\shape default
 setup however has some limitations because the mount namespace is still the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 one and access to network resources will be partially or totally blocked because:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
in the 1st case:
 the firewall of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 zone blocks both the DNS resolution requests and the outbound connections;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
in the 2nd case:
 the DNS server(s) cannot be generally reached because the traffic is routed through the external VPN gateway and not directly via the physical network interfaces.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So,
 in both cases,
 remote administration would be dysfunctional and the actual setup needs to be adjusted as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the remote access network services is executed in an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
administration namespace
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (
\series bold
admin NS
\series default
) which is a combination of a mount and network namespaces:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
the mount namespace is derived from the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 namespace but the 
\family typewriter
/etc/resolv.conf
\family default
 file points to a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
admin network infrastructure
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

's DNS service;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the network namespace which routes all the traffic trough the admin network infrastructure
\series bold
.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
admin network infrastructure
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is set up like a zone's infrastructure (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "zone-infra"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
services are exposed via DNAT;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

moving
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 services to the correct administration namespace is handled via the dedicated 
\family typewriter
\series bold
padsi-do
\family default
\series default
 tool.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following figure illustrates this setup:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename network-overview-admin.png
	width 80col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Compared to the architecture presented in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "network-overview"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the network ranges of IP addresses is different (in the 10.202.0.0/16 range but starting from the end,
 or 192.168.128.0/24);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the administration namespace is set up when a process is started.
 Specifically:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
the network namespace always exist;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a mount namespace is actually started when a process is started (via the 
\family typewriter
padsi-do
\family default
 tool).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
the admin infra.
 is configured similarly as for end users usage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Notes
\bar default
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
configuring the actual services in an administration NS is not done by PADSI but must be performed by a system administrator (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "remote-access-config"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the administration namespaces remain unchanged when the padsi systemd system service restarts.
 However,
 the underlying infrastructure is restarted which means that any opened incoming connections may experience a little delay during which they will appear as non responsive while the infrastructure is set up.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Local administration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Local administration allows users to perform actions (command line or scripts) with elevated privileges controlled via the 
\family typewriter
sudo
\family default
 program.
 Running 
\family typewriter
sudo
\family default
 from a zone will not work as expected because zones loose the ability to elevate privileges via 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

setuid
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and because sudo's configuration is not available (as a security feature).
 Escaping the zone's network and mount namespaces is not possible (as it would be a huge Linux kernel vulnerability).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Local administration thus needs to be performed by executing 
\family typewriter
sudo
\family default
 from a program running in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 namespaces (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "Execution in the init ns"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 even once this is done,
 the 
\family typewriter
sudo
\family default
 program will actually be in the same situation as the remote access (1st case) with the same limitations,
 and so the setup also needs to be adjusted.
 The same solution is applied here as for the remote access,
 and 
\family typewriter
\series bold
padsi-do
\family default
\series default
 tool is also used for that purpose.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\family typewriter
padsi-do
\family default
 tool
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "padsi-do-tool"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This tool runs a command in a combination of namespaces:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the network namespace of the selected administration namespace (if none specified,
 tries to find it if PADSI only created one);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a new mount namespace based on the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 one where a dedicated 
\family typewriter
/etc/resolv.conf
\family default
 file points to the correct DNS resolver;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
relies on 
\family typewriter
sudo
\family default
 to filter which commands can actually be executed by the user;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
if running from a non root user,
 restarts itself using 
\family typewriter
sudo
\family default
.
\end_layout

