\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Configuration
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "configuration"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to benefit from PADSI's security,
 one should respect the following rules:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
define business activities which correspond to a set of network services and of files,
 and assign a zone to each of these activities;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\color red
never set up a zone which 
\series bold
at the same time
\series default
 allows unrestricted network access and access to sensitive information
\color inherit
.
 This ensures an attacker which is able to execute his own malicious code in any zone will either be able to communicate with his malicious code OR have access to internal networks and/or sensitive information,
 but not both.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
PADSI's configuration is composed of multiple files in the 
\family typewriter
/etc/padsi
\family default
 directory (which can be changed in the 
\family typewriter
padsi.service
\family default
 file if necessary):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the 
\family typewriter
padsi.conf
\family default
 file is the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

entry point
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of all the configuration,
 it contains the global settings.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the 
\family typewriter
*.zone
\family default
 files each describe a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

zone
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the 
\family typewriter
*.netres
\family default
 files each describe a set of nerwork resources;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the 
\family typewriter
*.vm 
\family default
files each describe a virtual machine;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the *.tsp files each describe a traffic shaper configuration.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Global configuration
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "global-configuration"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The global configuration file 
\family typewriter
padsi.conf
\family default
 is a JSON file containing the following keys:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"var-dir"
\family default
:
 points to the directory which will contain all non volatile PADSI related data such as user's files in the various zones,
 virtual machines resource,
 etc.
 It default to 
\family typewriter
/var/padsi
\family default
.
 (which is created the first time the PADSI systemd service is started)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"xdg-data-dirs"
\family default
:
 a list of XDG directories of the system (refer to the 
\family typewriter
XDG_CONFIG_DIRS
\family default
 variable as per 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "XDG Base Directory Specification"
target "https://specifications.freedesktop.org/basedir-spec/latest/"
literal "false"

\end_inset

);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"networking"
\family default
:
 specifies the global networking settings of PADSI,
 via the sub keys:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"host-network"
\family default
:
 network used by PADSI to create all the virtual network structure in the host's 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 namespaces.
 Defaults to 
\family typewriter
10.202.0.0/16
\family default
 and should be left unchanged unless this network collides with some other enterprise network settings.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"host-access"
\family default
:
 list of network flows allowed for applications in in 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 namespaces when PADSI is running (there should only be the minimum necessary in and out network flows).
 This section is composed of network rules (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "network-rules"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) as lists in the 
\family typewriter
"out-rules"
\family default
 (for outbound connections) and 
\family typewriter
"in-rules"
\family default
 (for inbound connections) sections immediately below 
\family typewriter
"host-access"
\family default
.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"clipboard"
\family default
:
 a list of copy/paste rules to prevent data being copied from some zones to be pasted by possibly malicious application executed in less secure zones.
 Each rule is made of the following attributes:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

descr
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\family default
:
 a description of the rule;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

action
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\family default
:
 the action to be done,
 among 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\family typewriter
deny
\family default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\family typewriter
allow
\family default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

rule
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\family default
:
 the actual rule,
 in the 
\family typewriter

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

<copy zone>[,< copy zone>,...] > <paste zone>[,<paste zone>,...]
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\family default
 format and where 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\family typewriter
*
\family default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 can be used to reference all defined zones.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
\color red
WARNING
\bar default
\color inherit
:
 if not blocked by configuration,
 copy/paste operations are allowed by default.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"xdg-default-zone"
\family default
:
 the name of the zone in which the user is expected to execute most programs.
 If not null,
 the windows of programs in that zone will not have any outline;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"firewall-logs-group"
\family default
:
 null to log firewall blocked attempts to syslog,
 or a group number (integer) to log through a netlink socket to the specified group (requires an additional system configuration to handle those logs,
 probably using the ulogd daemon);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"nozone-apps"
\family default
:
 list of applications,
 identified by their 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Desktop File ID"
target "https://specifications.freedesktop.org/desktop-entry-spec/latest-single/#desktop-file-id"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 which will be by default executed outside of any zone (they will be executed in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 namespaces).
 It should contain applications which don't allow to author files or require network resources like the calculator,
 the disk mounter,
 the GNOME shell's extensions manager or the help system.
 If null,
 then all applications will be considered to run outside of any zone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Zones configuration
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "zones-configuration"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each zone is defined by a single file named 
\family typewriter
<zone name>.zone
\family default
 (the zone name must start with a lower case letter and contain only lower case letters and numbers) which is a JSON file containing the following keys:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"friendly-name"
\family default
:
 the user friendly name of the zone;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"start-mode"
\family default
:
 how PADSI starts the zone,
 among:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

always
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\family default
:
 the zone's infrastructure and the applications' part is always started even if no user requested program is running in it (for faster startup,
 but consumes a bit of resources all the time);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

always-infra
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\family default
:
 the zone's infrastructure is always started but the and the applications' part is not (it will be started when the user needs to run a program in it);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

on-demand
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\family default
:
 the zone infrastructure and the applications' part are started only when the user starts a program in that zone.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"color"
\family default
:
 the color of the outline applied to windows of programs running in the zone,
 the 
\family typewriter

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

#rrggbb
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\family default
 color format,
 or null if no outline must be shown;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"options"
\family default
:
 specific options for the zone,
 among:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"DESKTOP-NOTIFICATIONS"
\family default
:
 if true,
 programs in the zone will be able to display notifications in the user's desktop.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
"
\family typewriter
DNS-BLOCKLIST
\family default
":
 if set,
 must point to a file containing blocked domains,
 imported for example from 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
target "https://github.com/StevenBlack/hosts"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and in the following format (which can be generated using the 
\family typewriter
helpers/generate-blocklist.sh
\family default
 script):
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "basicstyle={\footnotesize\ttfamily}"
inline false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

server:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

  local-zone:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bad.domain.com" refuse
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

  local-zone:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bad.domain2.com" refuse
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

  ...
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Note
\bar default
:
 this option is only useful if the zone actually performs any DNS resolution.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"DRM"
\family default
:
 if true,
 allow access to the Direct Rendering Manager (/dev/dri);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"FIDO2"
\family default
:
 if true,
 permits usage of FIDO2 authenticators (e.g.
 for WebAuthn);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"GPG-CARD"
\family default
:
 if true,
 permits usage of GPG cards (note that the GPG keys still need to be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

manually
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 imported in the zone's 
\family typewriter
$HOME/.gnupg
\family default
 directory);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"FUSE"
\family default
:
 if true,
 programs will be able to mount filesystems using FUSE;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"MEDIAS"
\family default
:
 if true,
 the contents of the 
\family typewriter
/media
\family default
 directory where mass storage medias are plugged will be accessible;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"MOUNT-POINTS"
\family default
:
 if not empty,
 identify directories or files which will be mounted in the zone's context.
 Mount points are listed as a pair of:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
a file path (directory or file) in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

init
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 mount namespace,
 a non full path (i.e.
 not starting at the root) will be interpreted relative to the configuration directory (
\family typewriter
/etc/padsi
\family default
 by default);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a file path where it will be mounted (read-only) in the zone's context,
 a non full path will be mounted in the user's home directory;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Note
\bar default
:
 when mounting directories,
 the mounted directory may also contain other files or sub-directories.
 This is typically the case for the 
\family typewriter
/etc
\family default
 directory which may contain other files or directories required by the applications which will run in the zone.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"NET_LOG_ONLY"
\family default
:
 if true,
 network restrictions are only logged but not actually enforced;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"PKCS11"
\family default
:
 allows one to specify a PKCS#11 driver (shared library) to use smartcards,
 the option must be specified as a dictionary with:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"driver-name"
\family default
:
 a user friendly name for the driver;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"driver-file"
\family default
:
 the path to the actual shared library file.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"PKI"
\family default
:
 allows to declare certification authorities' certificates as trust anchors.
 Must be specified as a dictionary where keys are the user friendly name for a CA and associated values are paths to the associated PEM encoded certificate;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"SCREEN-SHARE"
\family default
:
 true if programs in the zone will be able to share (i.e.
 stream,
 like in conference calls) the whole Linux session's screen or any window of any zone;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"WEB_REDIRECTION"
\family default
:
 allows to enable the Web redirection feature (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "Web-redirection"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 The configuration is a dictionary with:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"zones"
\family default
:
 a list of zone names where redirections can be proposed to the users.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"X11"
\family default
:
 if true,
 programs in the zone will be able to access the system-wide X11 system to display applications.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
\color red
WARNING
\bar default
\color inherit
:
 this poses a security risk as such applications will then be able to interfere with all the X11 programs of all the zones.
 It should thus only be enabled when absolutely required.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"network"
\family default
:
 specifies the network settings of the zone (set to 
\family typewriter
null
\family default
 to disable networking in the zone),
 as the following keys:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"traffic-shaper"
\family default
:
 name of the traffic shaper to use,
 if any.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"dns-resolvers"
\family default
:
 list of the DNS resolvers to use:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
like 
\family typewriter
["8.8.8.8",
 "1.1.1.1"]
\family default
 to use specific DNS resolvers,
 or
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
null
\family default
 to avoid querying any external DNS resolver (local static DNS resolution can still provided),
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
or [] (empty list) to use the system's configured DNS resolvers (NOT YET IMPLEMENTED).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
DNS over TLS (DoT) is also supported,
 for example 
\family typewriter
["86.54.11.213@853",
 "86.54.11.13@853"]
\family default
 to use the EU's DNS' privacy and security focused resolvers
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
DNS4EU ,
 refer to https://www.joindns4.eu/
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following syntaxes are supported to specify a single resolver:
 
\family typewriter
<IP address>[@<port>][@<protocol>]
\family default
 where the protocol can be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

DoT
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for DNS over TLS.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

out-rules
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\family default
:
 list of applicable outbound filtering rules for the zone,
 refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "network-rules"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 for the syntax of each rule.
 The filtering decision is the action specified in the first matching rule,
 and a deny if no rule matched.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"web-proxy"
\family default
:
 can be 
\family typewriter
null
\family default
 or can specify if a forwarding Web proxy need to be used (a non forwarding Web proxy has no use since all the filtering is already done by PADSI).
 This may be the case if the Web requests have to go though an already existing Web proxy.
 Specify the Web proxy using:
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"proxy"
\family default
:
 the actual proxy to use in the format 
\family typewriter
<ip address>:<port number>;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

out-rules
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\family default
:
 list of rules applied locally by the proxy before forwarding the requests to the Web proxy down the chain,
 refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "network-rules"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 for the syntax of each rule.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"mounts"
\family default
:
 specifies the mount points in the zone:
 directories in the user's home directory in the zone which point to directories either in the real user's home directory or in other zones.
 Each mount point in the zone (i.e.
 the directory in the zone,
 relative to the home directory) is a key to which the following attributes are associated:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"source"
\family default
:
 where the directory actually is;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"mode"
\family default
:
 read-write (
\family typewriter
"rw"
\family default
) or read-only (by default if not specified)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The directories used as keys (relative to the home directory) can contain variables in the form 
\family typewriter
{<XDG dir>}
\family default
 where the XDG dir can be "DESKTOP",
 "DOWNLOAD",
 "TEMPLATES",
 "PUBLICSHARE",
 "DOCUMENTS",
 "MUSIC",
 "PICTURES",
 "VIDEOS".
 For example,
 
\family typewriter
{DOWNLOAD}/Internet
\family default
 corresponds to the 
\family typewriter
Download/Internet
\family default
 directory in a English locale or 
\family typewriter
Téléchargements/Internet
\family default
 in a French locale
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Linux behaves differently than Windows and MacOS with regards to the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

standard
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 top level directories present in each user's home (or profile) directory:
 the name presented to the user via any UI is actually the real directory name where both Windows an MacOS present a translated name in the UI while keeping the real name of the directory in English,
 which can be confusing.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The directories used as sources (also relative to the home directory) can also contain variables in the form 
\family typewriter
{[<zone name>_]<XDG dir>}
\family default
 with the same XDG constants.
 If the zone name is not specified,
 then the directory in the actual user's home directory will be used.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example then,
 the following in a zone specifies that the 
\family typewriter
$HOME/Download/Internet
\family default
 (in an English locale) directory will actually be the 
\family typewriter
$HOME/Download
\family default
 directory of the zone 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

internet
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and its contents will be read-only:
 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "basicstyle={\footnotesize\ttfamily}"
inline false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

"{DOWNLOAD}/Internet":
 {
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

	"source":
 "{internet_DOWNLOAD}",
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    "mode":
 "ro"
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And the following in a zone specifies that the 
\family typewriter
$HOME/Documents
\family default
 (in an English locale) directory will actually be the real 
\family typewriter
$HOME/Documents
\family default
 directory of the user:
 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "basicstyle={\footnotesize\ttfamily}"
inline false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

"{DOCUMENTS}":
 {
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    "source":
 "{DOCUMENTS}"
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"apps"
\family default
:
 a list of applications which are expected to be used in the zone,
 as identified by their App ID (like 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

org.gnome.Terminal
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

).
 Other applications can still be used but there will be no launcher icon and the GNOME shell will not be able to associate the correct icon's outline.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"virtual-machines"
\family default
:
 contain the list of virtual machines which may be used (run by a user
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The installation and update of virtual machines is not taken into account here.
\end_layout

\end_inset

) in that zone,
 along with some more possible restrictions.
 Thus each entry will be the VM's ID (defined as a 
\family typewriter
<vm ID>.vm
\family default
 file,
 refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "VM-conf"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 and the restrictions are specified via the 
\family typewriter
"specs"
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
"network"
\family default
 sections as described in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "VM-conf"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Traffic shaper
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "traffic-shaper"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each traffic shaper (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "routing-outside & traffic shaper"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) is defined by a single file named 
\family typewriter
<traffic shaper name>.tsp
\family default
 (the traffic shaper name must start with a lower case letter and contain only lower case letters and numbers) which is a JSON file containing the following keys
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A traffic shaper's configuration is a JSON object with the following keys:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"type"
\family default
:
 the actual traffic shaper type to use (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

wireguard
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 being the only supported for now);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"config"
\family default
:
 the configuration of the selected traffic shaper,
 as a JSON object.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
WireGuard traffic shaper
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The WireGuard traffic shaper accepts a the following configuration keys:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"file"
\family default
:
 points to the actual WireGuard configuration file (an INI style textual file) which:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\color red
must be protected to prevent any read or write by end users
\color inherit
;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
can be in any directory in the system if the specified path is absolute;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
is searched below PADSI's configuration directory (
\family typewriter
/etc/padsi
\family default
 by default) if its path is relative.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"ping-test"
\family default
:
 optional IP address to ping to actually determine if the VPN link is actually working as expected (testing will not be possible if absent or null);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
"interface"
\family default
:
 optional name the WireGuard interface will have (defaults to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

wg
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 if unspecified or null).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Network rules
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "network-rules"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two types of network rules:
 the simple one and the ones referencing a network resources set (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "netres"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 In both cases,
 there is a mandatory 
\family typewriter
"action"
\family default
,
 among 
\family typewriter
"allow"
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
"deny".
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Note
\bar default
:
 
\series bold
wherever network rules can be specified,
 the default rule for network flows which have not matched any rule will always be a deny.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Simple rules then also need:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
an optional description,
 used only to help in the rule's maintenance;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a definition of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

other
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 part of a connection,
 as either:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
a 
\family typewriter
"endpoint"
\family default
:
 IP addresses or CIDR,
 or domain names and possibly the transport means (TCP and/or UDP) and port numbers,
 like 
\family typewriter
"**.pool.ntp.org.
 ^ udp ^ 123"
\family default
 to represent any UDP connection on port 123 to any server ending in 
\family typewriter
.pool.ntp.org
\family default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a 
\family typewriter
"netres"
\family default
 a reference to a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

network resources set
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "netres"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
an optional 
\family typewriter
"resolv"
\family default
 section:
 a list of IP addresses the domain names will be resolved to,
 in the form 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

A/<response-validity>/<response as IPv4>
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 where:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

<response-validity>
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\family default
 is the TTL of the response (in seconds);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

<response as IPv4>
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\family default
 is the IPv4 address.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Below is an example of a list of network rules:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "basicstyle={\footnotesize\ttfamily}"
inline false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

[
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

	{
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

        
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

action":
 "allow",
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

        "descr":
 "Incoming SSH connection (not on the default port)",
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

        "endpoint":
 "* ^ tcp ^ 12345"
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    },
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    {
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

        "action":
 "allow",
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

        "netres":
 "testing"
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    },
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

	{
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

        "descr":
 "Test server",
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

        "action":
 "allow",
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

        "endpoint":
 "myserver.local.
 ^ tcp ^ 80,443",
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

        "resolv":
 [ "A/1200/192.168.1.4" ]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    }
