\begin_layout Subsection
Creating a VM disk image
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two possibilities to create a VM disk image (and its associated NVRAM file):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
use the usual toolset,
 install the VM (manually or automatically depending on the toolset),
 export the VM disk image file and then import it in a PADSI system;
 or
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
perform a VM installation on a PADSI system,
 and use the VM disk image.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In both cases,
 the PADSI VM agent,
 which is required to perform per user VM customization and proper setup (like shared directories) must be installed in the VM.
 The agent's installation package should be present in the 
\family typewriter
packages/
\family default
 directory if the installation (usually 
\family typewriter
/usr/share/padsi/packages
\family default
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
VM import from an externally created VM
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using non PADSI tools to install a VM or export a VM is possible,
 provided that:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the file format of the VM's disk image is 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "QCow"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qcow"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the VM's associated NVRAM file is also present.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Import
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To actually import the VM,
 use the 
\family typewriter
padsi-cli vm-import
\family default
 command (which may take some time depending on the disk image file size).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Post installation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Use the 
\family typewriter
padsi-cli vm-publish
\family default
 command to promote this new installation as a base VM image so users can actually use it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
VM installation using PADSI's tools
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Installing a VM via PADSI's tools required that the VM be first defined in the configuration,
 and specifically that any required network accesses during the installation be identified (at least to download any security updates to make sure the installed VM is up to date).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If any,
 PADSI will ensure these network accesses are available before the VM boots on its installation media:
 it will try to find a zone's network compatible with the requirements
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that only the zone's network is actually used as a communication means,
 and restricted to the declared VM's network requirements;
 the VM will also not be able to access any files accessible to the user in the zone.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (the user can also specify a zone's network to use,
 in which case PADSI will ensure its compatibility with the VM's requirements).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Installation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To actually start the VM installation,
 one needs the OS's installation media (ISO file,
 to be downloaded from the OS's manufacturer or project),
 and use the 
\family typewriter
padsi-cli vm-install
\family default
 command which opens the VM viewer and allows the actual VM installation,
 like:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename VM install.png
	width 75col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Notes
\bar default
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
if the zone's network requires the usage of a Web proxy,
 the administrator will have to specify this proxy during the installation as 
\family typewriter
http://proxy:3128
\family default
 (for HTTP and HTTPS connections);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
for proper operations,
 PADSI's VM agent must also be installed (refer to section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "VM agent"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) and enabled.
 The associated packages are automatically made available during installation via a virtual CD-ROM.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once the installation is complete,
 the system should be properly shut down (if the installation is not Ok,
 then the VM can simply be discarded),
 leaving a VM disk image (and its associated files) in the 
\series bold
staged
\series default
 state.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Post installation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Use the 
\family typewriter
padsi-cli vm-publish
\family default
 command to promote this new installation as a base VM image so users can actually use it.
\end_layout

